From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 15:33:44 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 11/14] &nf -l: load-aware delays in area recovery; delay
 rounds under updated loads (-Y)

Two defects behind the doubled depth of every load-aware mapping:

1. Only the delay round used the load-aware pin delay; the five area-
   recovery sites (Nf_ManSetMapRefsGate, Nf_MatchRef_rec, Nf_ManElaBest-
   MatchOne, Nf_ManComputeArrival, Nf_ManUpdateStats) still used the raw
   genlib pin delay, so required-time checks during area recovery saw
   a fraction of the real per-stage delay and filled paths with cheap deep chains. All go
   through Nf_CellPinDelay now (Nf_ManComputeArrival takes the root id).

2. The round-0 loads are a flow estimate; the loads of the mapping it
   produces differ, the mapping's arrivals then violate the required
   times ("Critical delay violation") and MapDelay relaxes
   to the violated value, giving area recovery far more slack than
   intended. New -Y <n>
   (default 2): re-run delay-oriented rounds under the previous mapping's
   loads, then freeze the loads for the area rounds (fFreezeLoads); the
   exact-area rounds keep their (Iter - nRounds) indexing.

On a cv64a6 group the load-aware mapping went from nearly twice stock's
logic depth to within about 10% of it at the same area (-Y 2 -P 25).
Stock path byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
